4. LIBRARY — PERSONAL STUDY MATERIALS

The Library is the student's personal academic knowledge base.

Students should be able to bring their existing study materials into the application instead of manually rewriting everything.

Supported inputs

Support importing:

- PDF documents.
- PowerPoint presentations.
- Photographs of textbook pages.
- Photographs of handwritten notes.
- Scanned documents.
- Typed notes.
- Pasted text.
- Other text-based academic materials where technically feasible.

Material ingestion pipeline

Implement a real ingestion process.

Step 1 — Import

Allow the student to upload or capture material.

Step 2 — Extract

Extract text from the material using the appropriate method:

- PDFs: extract selectable text directly first.
- Scanned PDFs: use OCR or AI vision when needed.
- PowerPoint: extract slide text and preserve slide boundaries.
- Images: use OCR or vision.
- Handwritten notes: use AI vision to interpret handwriting.

Do not unnecessarily send a readable PDF through vision when direct text extraction is sufficient.

Step 3 — Preserve structure

Preserve the original document's structure as much as possible:

- Headings.
- Subheadings.
- Paragraphs.
- Lists.
- Definitions.
- Examples.
- Tables where possible.
- Page numbers.
- Slide numbers.
- Section boundaries.

Do not turn a structured document into one giant unformatted text string.

Step 4 — Preview

Show the extracted content to the student before final saving.

Students must be able to:

- Read the extracted content.
- Edit it.
- Correct OCR errors.
- Fix inaccurate handwriting interpretation.
- Remove unwanted sections.
- Confirm the content.

If handwriting is unclear, do not confidently invent words. Mark uncertain interpretations and allow the student to correct them.

Step 5 — Organize

The student can assign:

- Course.
- Optional topic.
- Material title.
- Material type.

Step 6 — Save

Save the extracted and corrected text, metadata, and structural information to Firebase Firestore.

The default approach should be to store the extracted text and useful metadata rather than permanently storing unnecessarily large original files.

Original files may be temporarily used during extraction but should be discarded after processing unless the application has a clear reason to retain them.

Library management

Implement:

- Add material.
- View material.
- Edit extracted content.
- Rename material.
- Search materials.
- Assign and reassign course/topic.
- Add personal annotations.
- Delete material.
- Browse by course.
- Browse by topic.
- Browse by material type.

Library data structure

Each material should include appropriate metadata:

- ID.
- User ID.
- Course ID.
